{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following GNU C++ code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the GNU C++ code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n#include<new>\n#include<map>\n#include<set>\n#include<list>\n#include<queue>\n#include<cmath>\n#include<vector>\n#include<cstdio>\n#include<cstdlib>\n#include<cstring>\n#include<utility>\n#include<iostream>\n#include<algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\nbool judge;\nstring a,b;\n\nint main()\n{\n    cin>>a;\n    b+=a[0];\n    int i=0;\n    while(i<a.size()) {\n        if(a[i]=='d') {\n            if(i+2<a.size() && a[i+1]=='o' && a[i+2]=='t') {\n                b+='.';\n                i+=3;\n            } else {\n                b+=a[i];\n                i++;\n            }\n        } else if(a[i]=='a') {\n            if(i+1<a.size() && a[i+1]=='t' && !judge) {\n                b+='@';\n                i+=2;\n                judge=true;\n            } else {\n                b+=a[i];\n                i++;\n            }\n        } else {\n            b+=a[i];\n            i++;\n        }\n    }\n    cout<<b<<endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n#include <vector>\n#include <climits>\nusing namespace std;\n\nstring a;\nstring result;\nbool hasAt = false;\n\nint main() {\n    cin >> a;\n    \n    // We need to find exactly one '@' and replace 'at' with '@'\n    // For dots, replace 'dot' with '.'\n    // But we need the shortest possible result, and if tied, lexicographically smallest\n    \n    // First, let's find all possible positions for 'at' and 'dot'\n    vector<int> atPositions;\n    vector<int> dotPositions;\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)a.size() - 1; i++) {\n        if (a[i] == 'a' && a[i+1] == 't') {\n            atPositions.push_back(i);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < (int)a.size() - 2; i++) {\n        if (a[i] == 'd' && a[i+1] == 'o' && a[i+2] == 't') {\n            dotPositions.push_back(i);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Try each possible 'at' position (since we need exactly one '@')\n    string bestResult = \"\";\n    int minLength = INT_MAX;\n    \n    for (int atPos : atPositions) {\n        string candidate = \"\";\n        bool valid = true;\n        bool foundAt = false;\n        \n        // Process the string up to 'at'\n        for (int i = 0; i < atPos; i++) {\n            if (i < (int)a.size() - 2 && a[i] == 'd' && a[i+1] == 'o' && a[i+2] == 't') {\n                candidate += '.';\n                i += 2; // skip 'ot'\n            } else {\n                candidate += a[i];\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Add '@'\n        candidate += '@';\n        foundAt = true;\n        \n        // Process the string after 'at'\n        for (int i = atPos + 2; i < (int)a.size(); i++) {\n            if (i < (int)a.size() - 2 && a[i] == 'd' && a[i+1] == 'o' && a[i+2] == 't') {\n                candidate += '.';\n                i += 2; // skip 'ot'\n            } else {\n                candidate += a[i];\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Check if valid email\n        if (!foundAt) valid = false;\n        if (candidate[0] == '.' || candidate[0] == '@') valid = false;\n        if (candidate.back() == '.' || candidate.back() == '@') valid = false;\n        \n        // Count '@' symbols\n        int atCount = 0;\n        for (char c : candidate) {\n            if (c == '@') atCount++;\n        }\n        if (atCount != 1) valid = false;\n        \n        // Check for consecutive dots or other invalid patterns\n        for (int i = 0; i < (int)candidate.size() - 1; i++) {\n            if (candidate[i] == '.' && candidate[i+1] == '.') {\n                valid = false;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        if (valid) {\n            if (candidate.length() < minLength || \n                (candidate.length() == minLength && candidate < bestResult)) {\n                minLength = candidate.length();\n                bestResult = candidate;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // If no valid replacement found with 'at', try to handle edge cases\n    // But problem states there is always a solution, so we assume at least one 'at' exists\n    \n    cout << bestResult << endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-3.4570634852570947e-06, -0.00012909532233607024, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -7.629365427419543e-06, -0.639187753200531, -0.02189347892999649, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -1.5139465176616795e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.048789892345666885, -1.0132738680113107e-05, -0.06197422370314598, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.017904797568917274, -0.00029595286468975246, -0.0889546126127243, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -0.5830066800117493, -0.00019643761334009469, -0.0052352952770888805, -0.023275844752788544, -4.768370445162873e-07, -2.264974000354414e-06, -8.344646857949556e-07, -0.3551444709300995, -1.013344168663025, -0.5047324895858765, -0.33377787470817566, -0.23442044854164124, -0.5850675702095032, -0.11198252439498901, -0.7812068462371826, -0.03619253635406494, -0.6875842809677124, -2.622600959512056e-06, -0.14268366992473602, -0.7612605690956116, -0.0004033228906337172, -0.002476959954947233, -8.344646857949556e-07, 0.0, -0.0008662762120366096, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -2.1457441107486375e-05, -0.023248475044965744, -8.344646857949556e-07, -0.4142149090766907, -0.5880846977233887, -0.03838678076863289, -0.00043394684325903654, -0.10269637405872345, -0.3968296945095062, -5.07818695041351e-05, -0.1823260635137558, -5.4834770708112046e-05, -0.14436915516853333, -0.9523890018463135, -0.46810775995254517, -0.0014686522772535682, -4.95898348162882e-05, -0.013016965240240097, -0.0430605448782444, -0.39682480692863464, -6.6756979322235566e-06, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -1.0285619497299194, -0.06567005068063736, -0.018182972446084023, -0.5232837200164795, -0.0007186928996816278, -6.782778655178845e-05, 0.0, -5.304672595229931e-05, -0.7461463809013367, -0.000200609109015204, -0.07889085